{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Measured: the root filesystem is refilling on a sustained linear trend, and the docker restart did not arrest it.\n\n`root_disk_used_percent`, namespace `rebar/host`, half-hour averages over 20:41 UTC Sep 4 -> 08:11 UTC Sep 5 (11.5h):\n\n    20:41  48.6%      02:41  58.5%\n    21:41  47.0        03:41  57.4\n    22:41  48.8        04:41  59.8\n    23:41  50.6        05:41  59.5\n    00:41  52.0        06:41  58.8\n    01:41  55.8        07:11  62.0\n    02:11  59.8        08:11  68.0\n\nFirst-to-last: **48.6% -> 68.0%, +1.69 points/hour sustained.** Extrapolating that slope, the 85% alarm is reached in roughly 10 hours and the filesystem is full in roughly 19.\n\nTwo things make this ticket's business rather than a new bug.\n\n**The restart did not change the slope.** The scheduled `systemctl restart docker` at 05:23 UTC shows as a ~1-point dip (59.5 -> 58.6 across the two adjacent samples) and nothing more; growth resumes at the same rate immediately after, +9.4 points in the following 5.5 hours. So the reclaim was real but one-shot, and whatever is producing the growth is still producing it. That is direct evidence for this ticket's AC4 framing: a one-off sweep is not a ceiling, and the bounded ceiling is what is actually missing.\n\n**The caps that would bound it are not in service.** Confirmed by their own metrics rather than assumed: `docker_buildkit_cache_used_percent` is pinned at 99-100% of its 5 GiB budget (that cap IS working — the number is bounded), while `docker_unaccounted_bytes` climbed 2.03 -> 3.79 GB in the last 40 minutes against a 2 GiB threshold. The growth is escaping through the space the container-layer and scratch caps close, not through the budget already installed. Epic 6202's S3/S4/S5 are the caps in question and none has landed.\n\nHeadroom check, so this is not read as more urgent than it is: `disk_pressure_persists` is 0 across every sample, `docker_storage_used_percent` is 70%, `journal_used_percent` is flat at 57%. Nothing is degrading right now. The finding is a trajectory, not an active incident, and there is most of a working day of margin.\n\nOne correction against my own first pass, recorded because it changes what the numbers mean. I initially found no root-filesystem metric at all — `disk_used_percent` publishes only for `mount=/var/gerrit` — and was about to file the absence of root disk observability as a monitoring gap. That would have been wrong: root is covered by a separate dimensionless metric named `root_disk_used_percent`. The gap I nearly reported does not exist; the trend above is what the existing instrumentation was already showing.\n\nNot proposing an intervention here. Pruning or restarting again would repeat the one-shot reclaim this comment shows to be insufficient, and any deeper action on the production host is an operator call."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788597727836581708,"uuid":"8a227465-3998-4bc7-94cd-7c8c9ce011b5"}